4. System Architecture and Technical Specification

The architecture is implemented as five layers, each with formal interface contracts expressed in terms of state‑space bounds and timing constraints.

Layer 1 — Biometric Monitoring Shell (BMS)

· 64‑channel EMG array, 2 kHz, 16‑bit, <5 ms latency.
· 9‑DOF IMU network, 500 Hz.
· Sensor fusion via an extended Kalman filter providing body‑segment kinematics at 1 kHz.
· Mass: 0.8 kg.

Layer 2 — Powered Exoskeleton Chassis (PEC)

· 14‑DOF quasi‑direct drive (QDD) motors.
· Joint torque specs as in original, but now backed by a proof that the chosen actuators meet the peak power requirement derived from the dynamic AMB optimisation.
· Impedance controller with adaptive stiffness, proven ISS under bounded human force perturbations.
· Mass: 12 kg (target).

Layer 3 — Structural Protective Shell (SPS)

· Hybrid CFRP/Kevlar/Ti‑6Al‑4V, topology optimised using the mass lower bound theorem.
· Integrated radiator panels with PCM wax (Tm = 60 °C).
· Mass targets: Earth 18 kg, Lunar 24 kg, Martian 22 kg, all justified by the structural mass theorem.

Layer 4 — Propulsion and Energy System (PES)

Generation 0 (Earth demo): 5 kerosene micro‑turbines, total thrust 2 kN, P_{\text{jet}} \approx 200 kW, fuel mass 12 kg.
Generation 2 (Lunar): Cold‑gas N₂O thrusters, I_{sp}=180 s, 400 N thrust, propellant mass 15 kg for 45‑minute EVA.
Generation 3 (Earth battery): Solid‑state battery \rho_E \ge 1{,}500 Wh/kg, 22 kg; distributed electric ducted fans, each with inlet area optimised via momentum theory.
All variants satisfy the bifurcated propulsion model and maintain EAI(t)≥1 over the design mission.

Layer 5 — Human‑Machine Interface and AI Co‑Pilot (HMI‑AI)

· Reflex Controller: hard real‑time, proven schedulable.
· Motion Planner: runs at 100 Hz, uses a model‑predictive control (MPC) scheme with Lyapunov terminal cost.
· Adaptive mixing \alpha(t) governed by the ISS‑safe filter and cognitive‑load optimiser.
· AR helmet: 60°×40° field of view, latency <15 ms.

