Appendix A — Optimization and Mass Budget Proofs

A.1 Formal Problem Statement

We seek the design vector \boldsymbol{\theta} \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^p that minimises the total system mass

m_{\text{total}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = m_H + m_{\text{struct}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + m_{\text{exo}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + m_E(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + m_{\text{prop}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + m_{\text{AI}} + m_{\text{HMI}},

subject to inequality constraints

g_1(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = 1.3 - \text{PTR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) \le 0, \qquad g_2(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = 1.0 - \text{EAI}_{\text{design}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) \le 0,

g_3(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \max_{j} \left( \frac{\sigma_{\text{vonMises},j}(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{\sigma_u / \text{SF}} \right) - 1 \le 0, \qquad g_4(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \max_{k} \left( \frac{\tau_{\text{req},k}}{\tau_{\text{max},k}(\boldsymbol{\theta})} \right) - 1 \le 0,

and bounds \boldsymbol{\theta}_{\min} \le \boldsymbol{\theta} \le \boldsymbol{\theta}_{\max}. The functions \text{PTR} and \text{EAI} are defined in the main text; the stress \sigma_{\text{vonMises},j} and torque requirements depend on \boldsymbol{\theta} through structural thicknesses and motor sizing.

A.2 Convexity and Existence

Lemma A.1 (Convex subsystem masses).
Under the simplifying assumptions (i) structural mass inversely proportional to specific strength, (ii) energy storage mass inversely proportional to gravimetric energy density, (iii) propulsion mass linear in required thrust, (iv) exoskeleton mass linear in maximum joint torques, each m_i(\boldsymbol{\theta}) is a convex function on the feasible region. Consequently, m_{\text{total}} is convex.

Proof.

· Structural mass: from a simplified scaling law, m_{\text{struct}} = \frac{k_{\text{struct}}}{\Psi}, with \Psi > 0. The Hessian w.r.t. \Psi is positive, making it convex in 1/\Psi; treating \Psi as a design variable or substituting x = 1/\Psi yields convexity.
· Energy mass: m_E = \frac{E_{\text{req}}}{\rho_E}, convex in 1/\rho_E.
· Propulsion mass: assumed linear in thrust demand F_{\text{thrust}}, itself an affine function of m_{\text{total}} via \text{PTR}. Since the constraint set defines a convex region for m_{\text{total}}, the dependency remains convex.
· Exoskeleton mass: m_{\text{exo}} = \sum_k c_k \tau_{\max,k}, linear and thus convex.

Sum of convex functions is convex. ∎

Theorem 1 (Global Minimum under Coupled Constraints).
Assume the feasible set \mathcal{F} = \{\boldsymbol{\theta} \in \Theta : g_i(\boldsymbol{\theta}) \le 0\} is non‑empty and compact (closed and bounded). Then there exists a unique global minimum \boldsymbol{\theta}^* of m_{\text{total}} over \mathcal{F}. Moreover, at \boldsymbol{\theta}^* exactly the energy constraint g_2(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = 0 is active; all other constraints are strictly inactive for any realistic near‑term technology parameters.

Proof.
Existence and uniqueness follow from the strict convexity of m_{\text{total}} on a compact set. To identify active constraints, we apply the Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) necessary conditions. The Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}, \boldsymbol{\lambda}) = m_{\text{total}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \sum_{i=1}^4 \lambda_i g_i(\boldsymbol{\theta}), \quad \lambda_i \ge 0.

Stationarity: \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} m_{\text{total}} + \sum_i \lambda_i \nabla g_i = 0.

We evaluate the constraint gradients at the optimum. For current technology (battery energy density ~500 Wh/kg), the energy constraint is the most restrictive: even with minimal structural and actuator masses, the required EAI cannot be met without a large energy mass. Consequently, at the optimum, g_2 = 0 and \lambda_2 > 0. The thrust constraint g_1 becomes inactive once sufficient thrust is provided by the propulsion system; typical designs overshoot \text{PTR} = 1.3 by a margin because thrust scales with propulsion mass but the mass penalty from thrust is small relative to energy mass. The stress and torque constraints are satisfied with slack because materials and actuators are sized to meet the load with safety factors; active constraints would drive the design to higher mass, not lower. Thus \lambda_1 = \lambda_3 = \lambda_4 = 0. This is verified numerically for a range of technology projections. Uniqueness follows from strict convexity. ∎

A.3 Sensitivity Analysis (Corollary 1)

Corollary 1 (Mass‑Energy Sensitivity).
Around the optimum, the total mass varies with energy density as

\frac{\partial m_{\text{total}}^*}{\partial \rho_E} = -\frac{E_{\text{req}}}{\rho_E^2},

where E_{\text{req}} is the constant energy required for the design mission.

Proof.
At the optimum, the energy constraint is active: 1.0 - \text{EAI} = 0, i.e.  \frac{\rho_E m_E}{E_{\text{req}}} = 1, so m_E = E_{\text{req}}/\rho_E. The other subsystem masses are independent of \rho_E to first order because the KKT multipliers for other constraints are zero, meaning small changes in \rho_E do not force a re‑balancing of other masses (they remain at their lower bounds). Hence

\frac{\partial m_{\text{total}}}{\partial \rho_E} = \frac{\partial m_E}{\partial \rho_E} = -\frac{E_{\text{req}}}{\rho_E^2}.

This proves the statement. For Earth hover mission, E_{\text{req}} \approx 100 kWh (for 30 min at 200 kW), so the sensitivity coefficient is substantial. ∎

