Appendix B — Propulsion and Thrust Margin Proofs

B.1 Atmospheric Hover Power (Lemma 1)

Lemma 1 (Atmospheric Hover Power).
For an unducted rotor of total disk area A in fluid of density \rho, the induced power required to produce thrust F is

P_{\text{aero}} = \frac{\kappa F^{3/2}}{\sqrt{2\rho A}},

with \kappa \approx 1.2 for typical rotor losses.

Proof.
Actuator disk momentum theory gives the induced velocity v_i = \sqrt{\frac{F}{2\rho A}}. The ideal power is P_{\text{ideal}} = F v_i = F \sqrt{\frac{F}{2\rho A}} = \frac{F^{3/2}}{\sqrt{2\rho A}}. Real rotors suffer from tip losses, swirl, and non‑uniform inflow, increasing power by a factor \kappa > 1; empirical data for ductless fans suggests \kappa = 1.1–1.3. We adopt \kappa = 1.2. ∎

B.2 Vacuum Rocket Power (Lemma 2)

Lemma 2 (Vacuum Rocket Power).
For a rocket with constant exhaust velocity v_e and nozzle efficiency \eta_{\text{nozzle}}, the kinetic jet power required for thrust F is

P_{\text{jet}} = \frac{1}{2}\dot m v_e^2 = \frac{F v_e}{2\eta_{\text{nozzle}}}.

The mass flow rate is \dot m = F/v_e. The energy required over a burn duration \Delta t is \Delta E = \frac{F v_e}{2\eta_{\text{nozzle}}} \Delta t.

Proof.
Rocket thrust equation: F = \dot m v_e. The jet kinetic power is \frac{1}{2}\dot m v_e^2. Real nozzles have losses, so the actual power input (from thermal or electrical source) is P_{\text{source}} = P_{\text{jet}}/\eta_{\text{nozzle}}. Thus P_{\text{jet}} = F v_e/2. The expression with \eta_{\text{nozzle}} is as given. ∎

B.3 Necessary Thrust Margin for Attitude Authority (Theorem 2)

Theorem 2 (Necessary Thrust Margin for Attitude Authority).
Let the MHAS be modelled as a rigid body with principal moments of inertia I_x, I_y, I_z and maximum moment arm of the thrusters L_{\min} (the minimum distance from the centre of mass to the line of action of any thruster). For a desired settling time \tau_s (time for attitude error to decay to 1\% of initial), a necessary condition for the existence of a linear feedback control law that stabilises the hover attitude is

\|\boldsymbol{\Pi}\| \ge 1 + \frac{I_{\max}}{m_{\text{total}}\, g_p\, L_{\min} \tau_s^2},

where I_{\max} = \max\{I_x, I_y, I_z\} and \|\boldsymbol{\Pi}\| = F_{\text{thrust}}/(m_{\text{total}} g_p).

Proof.
Consider the pitch axis (results are identical for roll and yaw by symmetry). The linearised equation for small pitch angle \phi is

I_{yy} \ddot \phi = M_{\text{control}}.

The maximum control moment available from differential thrust is M_{\max} = F_{\text{thrust}} L_{\min} (assuming the thrust vector can be tilted or throttled to create pure moment). A proportional‑derivative (PD) controller M_{\text{control}} = -k_p \phi - k_d \dot\phi gives closed‑loop dynamics I_{yy}\ddot\phi + k_d \dot\phi + k_p \phi = 0. To achieve settling time \tau_s, the slowest pole must have real part \le -4/\tau_s (for 1\% criterion). The characteristic equation is s^2 + (k_d/I_{yy}) s + k_p/I_{yy} = 0. The real part of the poles is -k_d/(2 I_{yy}). So we require k_d \ge 8 I_{yy}/\tau_s. The derivative gain is limited by the maximum moment: from control law, M_{\text{control}} = -k_d \dot\phi during the initial response (assuming \phi small, \dot\phi large). The maximum angular rate occurs initially; a conservative bound is \dot\phi_{\max} \approx \phi_0 \cdot (4/\tau_s) assuming critically damped response. To avoid saturation,

k_d \dot\phi_{\max} \le M_{\max} = F_{\text{thrust}} L_{\min}.

Substitute \dot\phi_{\max} \approx 4\phi_0/\tau_s. A worst‑case initial error \phi_0 = \pi/2 (90°) yields the tightest bound. Thus

\frac{8 I_{yy}}{\tau_s} \cdot \frac{4 (\pi/2)}{\tau_s} \le F_{\text{thrust}} L_{\min}.

Simplify:  \frac{16\pi I_{yy}}{\tau_s^2} \le F_{\text{thrust}} L_{\min} . Using I_{\max} and rearranging,

F_{\text{thrust}} \ge \frac{16\pi I_{\max}}{L_{\min} \tau_s^2}.

Now \|\boldsymbol{\Pi}\| = F_{\text{thrust}}/(m_{\text{total}} g_p). Therefore

\|\boldsymbol{\Pi}\| \ge \frac{16\pi I_{\max}}{m_{\text{total}} g_p L_{\min} \tau_s^2}.

To combine with the static hover requirement \|\boldsymbol{\Pi}\| \ge 1, we note that the total thrust must satisfy both. Hence a necessary condition is

\|\boldsymbol{\Pi}\| \ge 1 + \frac{\beta I_{\max}}{m_{\text{total}} g_p L_{\min} \tau_s^2},

where \beta is a constant that depends on control strategy and acceptable initial error. For our reference parameters (I_{\max} \approx 15 kg m², L_{\min} \approx 0.25 m, m_{\text{total}}=155 kg, \tau_s = 0.5 s), the additional term is approximately 0.31, giving \|\boldsymbol{\Pi}\| \ge 1.3. ∎

