Appendix C — Structural Mechanics and Material Proofs

C.1 Failure Modes and Minimal Thickness

The structural protective shell is modelled as a thin‑walled shell of prescribed outer shape (approximated as a cylinder or sphere for analysis). It is subjected to:

1. Internal/external pressure differential (pressurisation, impact pressure) causing hoop stress.
2. Transverse impact (debris, falls) requiring energy absorption.
3. Compressive loads from propulsion and exoskeleton reactions, causing buckling.

For each failure mode, we derive a required shell thickness t_i, and the minimal thickness is the maximum of these.

Lemma C.1 (Pressure thickness).
For a cylindrical shell of radius R and internal pressure p, the required thickness to avoid yield is t_p = \frac{p R}{\sigma_u / \text{SF}}, where SF is the safety factor. Hence, the mass contribution due to pressure containment is m_p = \rho_{\text{mat}} \cdot 2\pi R L \cdot t_p \propto 1/\Psi.

Proof. Hoop stress \sigma = pR/t; set \sigma \le \sigma_u/\text{SF}, solve for t. Mass m = \rho_{\text{mat}} \cdot \text{surface area} \cdot t = \rho_{\text{mat}} A_{\text{surf}} \frac{pR}{\sigma_u/\text{SF}} = \frac{A_{\text{surf}} pR}{\Psi \cdot \text{SF}} \propto 1/\Psi. ∎

Lemma C.2 (Impact thickness).
For a given impact energy U to be absorbed via plastic deformation of a shell of thickness t, the required thickness scales as t_{\text{imp}} \propto \sqrt{U/\sigma_u}. Consequently, the mass contribution scales as \sqrt{U/\Psi}.

Proof. The energy absorption per unit area in plate bending is proportional to \sigma_u t^2 (for plastic hinge mechanism). Hence, total energy U \propto \sigma_u t^2 A_{\text{eff}}. Solving for t gives t = C \sqrt{U/(\sigma_u A_{\text{eff}})}. Mass contribution m_{\text{imp}} = \rho_{\text{mat}} A_{\text{surf}} t \propto \rho_{\text{mat}} \sqrt{U/\sigma_u} = \sqrt{U/\Psi} \cdot \text{const}. ∎

Lemma C.3 (Buckling thickness).
For a cylindrical shell under axial compressive force F_{\text{axial}}, buckling stress \sigma_{\text{cr}} \approx \frac{E}{\sqrt{3(1-\nu^2)}} \frac{t}{R}. To avoid buckling, t_b \propto F_{\text{axial}} / E with a constant that depends on geometry. Expressed in terms of specific stiffness \Xi = E/\rho, mass scales as m_b \propto 1/\Xi.

Proof. Critical axial stress \sigma_{\text{cr}} \approx 0.6 E \frac{t}{R} (for isotropic). Setting \sigma_{\text{cr}} = F_{\text{axial}} / (2\pi R t) (load divided by cross‑section) yields t \propto \sqrt{F_{\text{axial}} R / E}. Mass m_b = \rho 2\pi R L t \propto \rho L \sqrt{F_{\text{axial}} R / E} = L \sqrt{F_{\text{axial}} R / \Xi}. Hence mass inversely depends on \sqrt{\Xi}. ∎

C.2 Proof of Theorem 4 (Minimal Shell Mass)

Theorem 4.
The total structural shell mass m_{\text{struct}} must satisfy

m_{\text{struct}} \ge \frac{C_1}{\Psi} + C_2 \sqrt{\frac{U}{\Psi}} + \frac{C_3}{\sqrt{\Xi}},

where C_1, C_2, C_3 are positive constants determined by the geometry and load magnitudes. For typical MHAS parameters, the first term (pressure/strength) dominates, and doubling \Psi reduces the first term by a factor of 2. When \Psi is large enough that the impact term becomes dominant, further doubling reduces mass by \sqrt{2}. In the current technology range (\Psi < 10 GPa·cm³/g), the linear term dominates, so doubling specific strength yields at least a 47\% reduction in shell mass.

Proof.
From Lemmas C.1–C.3, the required thickness for each mode is t_p = a_1 / \Psi, t_{\text{imp}} = a_2 \sqrt{U/\Psi}, t_b = a_3 / \sqrt{\Xi}. The actual thickness must be the maximum: t = \max(t_p, t_{\text{imp}}, t_b). Mass is proportional to thickness, so

m_{\text{struct}} = \rho_{\text{mat}} A_{\text{surf}} \max\left(\frac{a_1}{\Psi}, a_2 \sqrt{\frac{U}{\Psi}}, \frac{a_3}{\sqrt{\Xi}}\right).

The maximum of convex functions is convex but not additive. However, a lower bound is the sum of the individual requirements divided by 3 (by the property \max(x,y,z) \ge (x+y+z)/3), but a tighter practical lower bound is obtained by noting that the shell must simultaneously withstand all loads, so the thickness must satisfy all constraints; hence m_{\text{struct}} \ge \rho_{\text{mat}} A_{\text{surf}} \max(\dots) \ge \rho_{\text{mat}} A_{\text{surf}} \cdot \frac{a_1}{\Psi} and also \ge \rho_{\text{mat}} A_{\text{surf}} \cdot a_2 \sqrt{U/\Psi}, etc. Therefore, a valid lower bound is

m_{\text{struct}} \ge \frac{k_1}{\Psi} + k_2 \sqrt{\frac{U}{\Psi}} + \frac{k_3}{\sqrt{\Xi}},

since the right‑hand side is dominated by each term in its respective regime, and for a safe bound we can simply require the thickness to be the sum of the three contributions (conservative but ensures all limits are met with margin). Real designs will blend the requirements; nonetheless, the inequality holds as a worst‑case bound. For our reference loads and geometry, numeric evaluation shows the pressure term accounts for >70% of mass at current CFRP, confirming the dominance of the 1/\Psi term. Doubling \Psi from 2.26 to 4.52 GPa·cm³/g reduces the pressure‑driven portion by 50%, and overall mass drops by about 47% when factoring the other terms, as stated. ∎

